camchrome: every UA surface agrees with the User-Agent in effect, and with real versions.

Measured on 157.0.8103.78 before this patch:

  * --fingerprint-brand=edge: UA "Edg/157.0.0.0" but Sec-CH-UA / fullVersionList /
    uaFullVersion "Microsoft Edge" 150.0.4078.65 (a frozen table). Edge now reports the Edge
    stable build that ships on this Chromium major (fingerprint_data.h kEdgeStable,
    157 -> 157.0.4341.60 from edgeupdates.microsoft.com), in the UA token and every hint.
  * --fingerprint-brand=opera / vivaldi defaulted to Opera 126 / Vivaldi 7.7, releases built on
    a much older Chromium. They now need --fingerprint-brand-version; without it plain Chromium
    is claimed and a warning is logged.
  * --user-agent=<UA>: the string changed, the client hints kept the engine's version and OS
    (UA Chrome/150 on Windows next to Sec-CH-UA 157 on macOS).
  * CDP Emulation.setUserAgentOverride without userAgentMetadata (Puppeteer setUserAgent,
    Playwright userAgent): Sec-CH-UA disappeared from requests while navigator.userAgentData
    kept the engine's brands.
  * CDP override WITH userAgentMetadata: UpdateUserAgentMetadataFingerprint() ran afterwards and
    replaced the client's brands with the persona's; workers re-derived them independently.

Now (user_agent_fingerprint.h):
  * An explicit override is final. Consumers (client_hints.cc, navigator_ua.cc,
    frame_fetch_context.cc) pass whether one is active; only the browser's own metadata is
    fingerprinted. Fields a CDP client omits come from the fingerprinted default, and an omitted
    fullVersion follows the client's own fullVersionList.
  * ReconcileUserAgentMetadataWithUserAgent() makes the browser's own metadata agree with the UA
    string in effect: Chromium/Chrome major (full version: --fingerprint-brand-version if full,
    the engine's if the majors match, else <major>.0.0.0), the Edg/OPR/Vivaldi tokens, and the
    OS (Windows, macOS, Linux, Android incl. the Mobile bit).
  * A UA-only CDP override gets metadata derived the same way, on the browser side (headers) and
    the renderer side (navigator.userAgentData), so both agree.
  * Dedicated workers receive their creator's final metadata and do not re-derive it.
  * fingerprint_data.h no longer holds any Chrome version (they were 156 on a 157 engine and
    unused for Chrome); the persona's 151 table is documented as a seed-stability draw only.
  * Sec-CH-UA-Platform-Version: the table index used the raw --fingerprint text parsed as an int
    (non-numeric seeds always got entry 0; a negative seed indexed before the table). It now uses
    the persona seed. macOS platform versions were all 15.0-15.5; the table now holds released
    macOS 26 and 15.7 builds.

Tests: tools/ua_selftest.py (UA string, Sec-CH-UA, full-version list, userAgentData in page and
worker, for the default, persona, each brand, --user-agent, and CDP overrides with and without
metadata).
